/PEX5","cell_types":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.392722,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.060098,"nucleus_score":-0.973,"pct_ad":0.1251,"pct_control":0.127737,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.932072,"donor_log2fc":-0.0336604,"donor_lfcse":0.0758342,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00332938,"degas_log2fc":-0.175057,"degas_padj":8.86685e-05},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.253778,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0150888,"nucleus_score":1.18017,"pct_ad":0.57686,"pct_control":0.586219,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.830516,"donor_log2fc":0.0387468,"donor_lfcse":0.0601492,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0182055,"degas_log2fc":0.0417519,"degas_padj":1.07273e-09},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":3.67577e-05,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0491011,"nucleus_score":-4.21299,"pct_ad":0.32276,"pct_control":0.332373,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.888877,"donor_log2fc":0.0204215,"donor_lfcse":0.0390486,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00497543,"degas_log2fc":0.0648856,"degas_padj":3.08678e-07},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.752183,"donor_log2fc":0.109793,"donor_lfcse":0.104504,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0195584,"degas_log2fc":null,"degas_padj":null},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"unclear","nucleus_padj":0.973242,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0223719,"nucleus_score":0.0556734,"pct_ad":0.162392,"pct_control":0.161389,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":-0.00637318,"donor_lfcse":0.0970972,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-9.79201e-06,"degas_log2fc":-0.190769,"degas_padj":0.00817192},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"PEX5","gene_symbol_current":"PEX5","gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"unclear","nucleus_padj":0.984606,"nucleus_log2fc":0.00392632,"nucleus_score":-0.0230098,"pct_ad":0.10214,"pct_control":0.102141,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.932571,"donor_log2fc":0.0389169,"donor_lfcse":0.0728462,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00690322,"degas_log2fc":-0.176778,"degas_padj":2.19336e-06}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000139197","entrez_id":["5830"],"uniprot":["P50542"],"ncbi_summary":"The product of this gene binds to the C-terminal PTS1-type tripeptide peroxisomal targeting signal (SKL-type) and plays an essential role in peroxisomal protein import. Peroxins (PEXs) are proteins that are essential for the assembly of functional peroxisomes. The peroxisome biogenesis disorders (PBDs) are a group of genetically heterogeneous autosomal recessive, lethal diseases characterized by multiple defects in peroxisome function. The peroxisomal biogenesis disorders are a heterogeneous group with at least 14 complementation groups and with more than 1 phenotype being observed in cases falling into particular complementation groups. Although the clinical features of PBD patients vary, cells from all PBD patients exhibit a defect in the import of one or more classes of peroxisomal matrix proteins into the organelle. Defects in this gene are a cause of neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy (NALD), a cause of Zellweger syndrome (ZWS) as well as may be a cause of infantile Refsum disease (IRD). Alternatively spliced transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been identified. [provided by RefSeq, Oct 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P50542","text":"Receptor that mediates peroxisomal import of proteins containing a C-terminal PTS1-type tripeptide peroxisomal targeting signal (SKL-type) (PubMed:11101887, PubMed:11336669, PubMed:12456682, PubMed:16314507, PubMed:17157249, PubMed:17428317, PubMed:21976670, PubMed:26344566, PubMed:7706321, PubMed:7719337, PubMed:7790377). Binds to cargo proteins containing a PTS1 peroxisomal targeting signal in the cytosol, and translocates them into the peroxisome matrix by passing through the PEX13-PEX14 docking complex along with cargo proteins (PubMed:12456682, PubMed:17157249, PubMed:21976670, PubMed:26344566). PEX5 receptor is then retrotranslocated into the cytosol, leading to release of bound cargo in the peroxisome matrix, and reset for a subsequent peroxisome import cycle (PubMed:11336669, PubMed:24662292). [Isoform 1]: In addition to promoting peroxisomal translocation of proteins containing a PTS1 peroxisomal targeting signal, mediates peroxisomal import of proteins containing a C-terminal PTS2-type peroxisomal targeting signal via its interaction with PEX7 (PubMed:11336669, PubMed:11546814, PubMed:25538232, PubMed:33389129, PubMed:9668159). Interaction with PEX7 only takes place when PEX7 is associated with cargo proteins containing a PTS2 peroxisomal targeting signal (PubMed:25538232). PEX7 along with PTS2-containing cargo proteins are then translocated through the PEX13-PEX14 docking complex together with PEX5 (PubMed:25538232). [Isoform 2]: Does not mediate translocation of peroxisomal import of proteins containing a C-terminal PTS2-type peroxisomal targeting signal."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}